/* ============================================================================
   gcd-of-a-list.epher -- the greatest common divisor of several numbers

   Reduces a list of numbers to one gcd by nesting the two-argument
   builtin: gcd(gcd(a, b), c) divides a, b and c, and no larger number
   does, because gcd is associative. The script demonstrates the builtin;
   it does not reimplement it. The checks: the numbers 48, 36 and 60
   share the factor 12, and 14, 21 and 28 share 7.

   Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
   ch. 1 (the gcd, and its associativity). gcd is exact.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/divisors/gcd-of-a-list.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\divisors\gcd-of-a-list.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/divisors/gcd-of-a-list.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the sample lists are the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- reductions of three numbers at a time --------------------------------------
print("gcd of 48, 36 and 60:", gcd(gcd(48, 36), 60))      // gcd(12, 60) = 12
print("gcd of 24, 36 and 60:", gcd(gcd(24, 36), 60))      // gcd(12, 60) = 12
print("gcd of 14, 21 and 28:", gcd(gcd(14, 21), 28))      // gcd(7, 28) = 7
print("gcd of 15, 25 and 40:", gcd(gcd(15, 25), 40))      // gcd(5, 40) = 5
print("gcd of 12, 18 and 30:", gcd(gcd(12, 18), 30))      // gcd(6, 30) = 6

// ---- nested one step deeper -------------------------------------------------------
// Four numbers reduce the same way, folding from the left.
print("gcd of 8, 12, 16 and 20:", gcd(gcd(gcd(8, 12), 16), 20))  // 4
print("gcd of 6, 10, 15 and 21:", gcd(gcd(gcd(6, 10), 15), 21))  // 1: coprime group
/* ---- expected output ----
= gcd of 48, 36 and 60: 12
= gcd of 24, 36 and 60: 12
= gcd of 14, 21 and 28: 7
= gcd of 15, 25 and 40: 5
= gcd of 12, 18 and 30: 6
= gcd of 8, 12, 16 and 20: 4
= gcd of 6, 10, 15 and 21: 1
*/
